# {{ identity.display_name }} knowledge base

A code-verified knowledge base for {{ identity.display_name }} projects, loaded as background context while you develop. When you work on a project or business flow, the matching reference gives you its architecture, dependencies, deployment, environments and cross-project conventions before you touch code, so you (or the assistant) do not rediscover them or guess from training data.

It is not a Q&A bot or a tutorial. It is the map you read before going in: where things are, how they connect, and what traps to avoid.

## Philosophy

The value of this knowledge base is that it is trustworthy, not that it covers everything. A single stale fact gets quoted as authoritative and quietly drags down the credibility of the whole base: wrong information is worse than none. Every rule follows from that:

- Trust over coverage. Scope is deliberately narrow; the base does not try to document anything you could look up.
- Do not duplicate code. Anything an assistant can re-derive by reading the current repo (package lists, folder layouts, route tables) is left to the repo. Copying it in only creates a second thing to keep correct.
- Record only what code cannot tell you: cross-project relationships, deployment topology, environments, naming caveats, "looks like a bug but is intentional", why it is built this way, and who to find when it breaks.
- Anchor every fact so staleness is cheap to catch. Claims carry grep-able code anchors, dated references to non-code sources, or one of the markers `(unverified)`, `(human: …)`, `(external: …)`. Unmarked text means verified against code. The goal is not to pick facts that never change; it is that a sweep can tell whether a fact still holds.
- Audit, not authoring, is the credibility mechanism. Writing a doc makes it correct now; the routine audit against the repos keeps it correct over time.

## What's inside

Two knowledge blocks{{#if has.observability_doc}}, a shared observability reference{{/if}}{{#if has.shared_catalogs}} and a cross-cutting catalog{{/if}}, all under `skills/{{ names.knowledge }}/references/`:

| Entry | Contents |
|---|---|
| `project/index.md` | Per-project basics: purpose, architecture, dependencies, deployment, environments, investigation entry points. One doc per project in scope. |
| `domain/index.md` | Cross-project end-to-end business flows, business rules and the domain glossary. |
{{#if has.observability_doc}}
| `observability.md` | Investigation: conventions for {{ derived.observability_tools }} shared across projects. |
{{/if}}
{{#if has.shared_catalogs}}
| `platform-basics.md` | Cross-cutting lookups: {{ derived.catalog_headings }}. |
{{/if}}

Project and domain docs cross-link: a project doc links to the flows it takes part in, and each flow lists the projects it touches, so you can hop from a service to the end-to-end flow and back. The two `index.md` files are the source of truth for the inventory; this README deliberately does not repeat counts or lists that would go stale.

## How it is used

The reader skill `{{ names.knowledge }}` loads itself as context when you develop, debug, review or plan work on a project or flow, or when you name one. You can also load it on demand by naming it. `SKILL.md` carries the always-on platform basics; for anything project- or flow-specific, follow the relevant `index.md` and load only the part you need. To make the reader skill available to Claude Code, link or copy the folder `skills/{{ names.knowledge }}/` into a `.claude/skills/` directory, the same way the maintainer kit is linked (see "Contributing a doc"). You can also read the files under `skills/{{ names.knowledge }}/references/` directly.

## What you unlock at each tier

The knowledge base works on its own and gets more capable as you add tooling around it. Each tier is useful by itself; combining them compounds.

| You have | You can |
|---|---|
| the knowledge base alone | Ask about any project or flow (architecture, dependencies, environments, flows, naming traps) and be answered from the docs, with no repo or extra tool needed. |
{{#if has.live_access}}
| plus live tools: {{ lists.live_access }} | Resolve the live values the docs deliberately do not store. |
{{/if}}
| plus local repos under `{{ paths.repos_root }}` | Verify facts against current code, and let the maintainer kit audit and refresh the whole base against your clones. |

## Routing for your CLAUDE.md

`CLAUDE.snippet.md` holds a short block to paste into the CLAUDE.md of the machine or project that reads this knowledge base. The reader skill loads lazily from its description; tools and MCP servers do not, so the block says when to reach for each.

## Scope

{{ scope.rule }} Each project doc records its owning team from this vocabulary: {{ lists.owners }}. Pure upstream dependencies that are only called, not developed, are listed by name and get no doc of their own.

## Maintaining the knowledge

The authoring kit is a set of Claude Code skills under [`local-tools/`](./local-tools/). It is not a reader skill: nothing loads it from there, and every skill in it is command-only. It holds the maintainer skills and the shared authoring rules:

- `{{ names.add_project }}`: add or update a per-project doc
- `{{ names.add_domain }}`: add or update a domain flow doc
- `{{ names.sync_local }}`: audit and refresh the docs against local repos under `{{ paths.repos_root }}`
- `{{ names.verify }}`: review a contributor's changeset before accepting it
- `{{ names.list_markers }}`: list every open marker as a hand-off worklist
- `{{ names.update_skills }}`: edit the kit itself and relink it

See [`local-tools/README.md`](./local-tools/README.md) for the maintainer workflow.

### Contributing a doc

You do not need every repo cloned, or to be an engineer: the skills route each fact by what you have, not by who you are.

The steps live in [`local-tools/README.md`](./local-tools/README.md): "Activate" links the maintainer skills into `{{ kit.link_dir }}` (a fresh clone has none), and "Contributing a change" covers authoring with `/{{ names.add_project }}` or `/{{ names.add_domain }}`, the merge request, and the maintainer's `/{{ names.verify }}` before merging. A contribution is a lead, not a merge-ready fact.
